; SCENE_test_zone_W2's terrain, textured from the `textures/` photo set.
;
; Replaces MAT_terrain_flatcolor.tres in the scene; that material is still here
; and still tested, so flipping back to the flat graphic look is a one-line scene
; change. Everything below the texture assignments -- the fog band, the exact
; neutral fog colour, the smooth shading -- is carried over from it unchanged,
; because those numbers are tied to the sky material and the palette LUT rather
; than to how the ground is painted. See docs/DOC_scene_w2_skybox_terrain.md.
;
; Layer choice, and what is deliberately left unused. `textures/` ships variants
; for four of the seven layers; this picks one of each and leaves the rest as
; alternates to swap in:
;   rough        TEX_idealized_grass (drawn blades, not a photo; see the grass
;                                    section below -- alternate: forest_ground_01,
;                                    the moss-and-twigs photo this replaced)
;   fairway      lawn_striped       (mower stripes read as maintained from the air)
;   soil / pad   dirt_01
;   forest floor forest_ground_06   (dark needle litter)
;   path         rocky_trail_02     (alternate: rocky_trail_01)
;   rock         dark_rock_02       (fractured slabs; alternate: dark_rock_01,
;                                    which is smoother and reads as marble on a
;                                    cliff face)
;   sand         coast_sand_02      (alternates: coast_sand_01, greener;
;                                    aerial_beach_01, flat and grey)
;
; TINTS ARE LINEAR COLOUR MULTIPLIERS, not colours -- the uniform is a plain vec3
; on purpose, because several channels have to be pushed above 1.0. Each is set
; PER CHANNEL so the layer's mean colour lands on the flat-colour palette entry it
; replaces, measured off the shipped texture through this project's import
; pipeline. The texture keeps every bit of its own variation; only its average
; moves.
;
; Matching the LEVEL alone is not enough, and that is the whole reason these are
; three numbers rather than one. A photo set is not graded to anything: the rough
; (forest_ground_01) averages olive-tan, and at the right brightness but the wrong
; hue the island rendered as a khaki dune field. It matters more here than it
; would elsewhere because `SHADER_post_dither.gdshader` snaps every pixel to one of
; sixteen palette entries -- a colour that lands between two of them does not
; blend, it jumps to whichever is nearer, so an un-graded hue does not read as
; "slightly off", it reads as a different surface.
;
;   layer   texture mean (lin RGB)     ->  flat palette entry
;   grass   0.027 0.110 0.023              (see the grass section below)
;   golf    0.145 0.267 0.011              (no longer a palette match; see below)
;   soil    0.126 0.086 0.049              0.107 0.060 0.026
;   forest  0.103 0.059 0.029              0.048 0.032 0.015
;   path    0.246 0.150 0.085              0.194 0.153 0.102
;   rock    0.043 0.034 0.025              0.102 0.105 0.117   very dark -> grey
;   sand    0.223 0.171 0.107              0.590 0.457 0.192
;
; THREE OF THEM ARE NOT A LITERAL MATCH, and the reason is clipping. Matching a
; mean per channel says nothing about where the HIGHLIGHTS land, and the ratio
; needed to move a mean is applied to the whole range: the rock's target is a
; near-neutral grey and the photo is warm brown, so a literal match wanted blue at
; 4.6x, which took the bright texels past white and painted the coastal rock band
; and the cliffs a pale blue-white. Golf did the same in reverse — lawn_striped has
; almost no blue, so lifting its mean to a vivid green needed 3.5x there and
; clipped the green channel outright, which is where the teal bunkersides came
; from.
;
; So: rock is NEUTRAL, keeping the rock's own warm hue instead of forcing it grey;
; golf and sand are pulled well back toward neutral. The rule is that a tint may
; move a layer's level freely and its hue only as far as the texture's weakest
; channel can take without clipping.
;
; THE WHOLE SET WAS RE-GRADED WHEN THE SUN CAME DOWN (3.0 -> 1.2, and ambient
; 1.8 -> 1.2 with it), and that is the more useful thing to know than any
; individual number here.
;
; These tints were originally anchored to the OLD FLAT-COLOUR PALETTE's values —
; targets chosen for a shader with no textures and no normal maps, where golf sat
; at 0.42 luminance and sand at 0.47 against a rough at 0.14. Matching them meant
; BRIGHTENING golf, sand and rock and darkening only the rough, so under a
; 3.0-energy sun every layer that had been pushed up clipped, and the one that had
; been pushed down was the only one that read correctly. Ground-level captures had
; the fairway as electric yellow-white and the cliff tops, grass and tree
; impostors as flat white.
;
; Lowering the exposure is the single-cause fix — the sky is a shader, the cloud
; decks are unshaded and both fogs are colour constants, so the sun only touches
; the four surfaces that were actually blowing out. What it leaves behind is this:
; golf and sand KEEP their tints (they were the ones clipping; the sun coming down
; is what fixes them), while the rough comes back up by roughly what the sun came
; down by, and the dark layers stop being crushed to compensate for a hot sun.
;
; The rule that survives all of it: a tint may move a layer's level freely and its
; hue only as far as the texture's weakest channel can take without clipping.
;
; THE GOLF LAYER IS GRADED FOR VIVIDNESS NOW, not for a palette match, and RED is
; the channel that carries it. `lawn_striped` is a photograph of real turf, so it
; is olive: 0.145 0.267 0.011, red already at 54% of green before any tint touches
; it. The old 1.05 / 1.5 / 1.35 raised the level and left that ratio where it
; found it -- rendered, the near fairway came out 174 177 79, which is red at 98%
; of green. That is not a green the tonemap desaturated, it is a YELLOW, and it is
; what "washed out" meant. Pulling red DOWN is what fixes it; pushing green up
; only makes a brighter version of the same colour.
;
; SWEPT, NOT GUESSED, because there is a wall on the far side of this and it is
; not clipping. Four tints rendered through the full scene at the `tee_stand`
; viewpoint (`tests/PROBE_golf_shots.gd`), measured over the near fairway:
;
;   tint                 turf RGB      R/G   stripe contrast   dominant entry
;   1.05 1.5  1.35    174 177  79      98%       11.2               52%
;   0.72 1.62 0.95    127 165  74      77%       15.8               38%
;   0.60 1.70 0.88    107 160  73      66%       10.3               59%   <- shipped
;   0.50 1.80 0.82     92 160  79      57%        5.8               90%
;
; THE WALL IS THE MOWER STRIPES. `lawn_striped` was chosen because the stripes
; read as deliberate maintained ground from the air, and they only read while the
; turf spans more than one palette entry. At 0.50 the fairway snaps to a single
; entry across 90% of its pixels and the stripes are gone -- a flat green card
; where a golf course was. 0.60 is the last stop before that: it holds essentially
; the shipped stripe contrast (10.3 against 11.2) while cutting red by a third.
; The 0.72 row's higher number is not more stripe, it is DITHER -- two entries
; splitting the surface 38/62, which reads as speckle rather than as mowing.
;
; The colour it lands on is exactly the palette entry 58a050, where the old tint
; landed on a0b860. Both are real ps1-soft entries -- this did not move the
; fairway off the palette, it moved it from the palette's yellow-green to its
; green.
;
; AND IT CLIPS NOTHING, which is the check this header exists to demand. Measured
; on `TEX_lawn_striped_albedo_128.png` through the sRGB->linear the import applies,
; the texture's per-channel maxima are 0.328 / 0.407 / 0.019, so the brightest
; texel in the set lands at 0.197 / 0.692 / 0.017 and 0 of 49,152 channels clip.
; The clipping this file warns about was measured against a 3.0-energy sun; the
; sun is a fake 0.25 now (`TimeOfDay.day_sun_energy` through
; `SHADERINC_island_light.gdshaderinc`) and the headroom is real. Note which way the rule
; bends here: the weakest channel is BLUE, and this change LOWERS it, so the
; constraint is respected in the safe direction rather than tested.
;
; THE GRASS LAYER IS NO LONGER ANCHORED TO THE PALETTE ENTRY IN THE TABLE ABOVE.
; It is anchored to the TUFT SPRITES that `SCRIPT_grass_scatter.gd` stands in it by the
; tens of thousands. The table's targets were chosen when the ground was the only
; green thing in the frame; it is not any more, and a target that was right in
; isolation is wrong the moment something else green is planted on top of it. The
; old tint left the ground markedly greener and less red than the tufts standing
; in it, so they read as objects LYING ON the turf rather than growing out of it.
;
; TWO THINGS MAKE THIS LAYER DIFFERENT FROM EVERY OTHER ROW IN THAT TABLE, and
; both were learned the hard way, so do not re-derive this one by matching texture
; means the way the others were.
;
; FIRST: THE MATCH HAS TO BE MADE IN RENDERED SPACE, NOT IN ALBEDO SPACE. The
; terrain is lit and the sprites are `unshaded` -- `SHADER_veg_billboard.gdshader` sends
; ALBEDO straight to the tonemapper. The sun and the near-neutral ambient multiply
; ONE side of the comparison and not the other, so a tint that matches the
; sprite's albedo chromaticity exactly still does not match it on screen. Matching
; the albedo was tried first and left a rendered-chromaticity gap of 0.055.
;
; SECOND: IT HAS TO CLEAR THE POST QUANTISATION TO EXIST AT ALL. `post_dither`
; snaps each channel to `lut_levels` = 16 steps BEFORE the palette lookup, so a
; correction smaller than one step (0.067 in sRGB) rounds straight back to the
; colour it started from. The albedo-space match moved sRGB red by 0.059 -- just
; under one step -- and rendered a frame that was, to the pixel, the one it was
; meant to fix: bare ground stayed 85% #58a050 either way. A hue correction here
; is either big enough to change the LUT cell or it is not visible.
;
; SO THIS NUMBER WAS SOLVED AGAINST THE RENDERER, not computed from the texture.
; `--no-postfx` captures of `tests/RENDER_w2_ground.gd --only=rough` give the
; ground's raw pre-palette colour at a known tint, and the tint is solved from
; those. Re-measure if the sun energy, the tonemapper or the palette ever moves;
; the fit is against this exposure and nothing else.
;
; THE HARD PART IS MEASURING THE GROUND AT ALL, not solving once you have it.
; The `rough` viewpoint is a fern-and-tuft floor, so most of the frame is sprite,
; and two obvious statistics both fail on it:
;
;   - k-means on the near band. The cluster boundary MOVES when the tint moves, so
;     each capture clusters a different pixel set and the numbers are not
;     comparable. It reported the ground getting GREENER after a change that cut
;     the green tint from 2.42 to 1.74.
;   - the histogram mode. It returns the single most common exact colour, which is
;     a flat `unshaded` sprite colour -- identical to four decimals across every
;     capture, because sprites do not see `grass_tint` at all.
;
; WHAT WORKS IS A DIFFERENCE MASK. Render the same viewpoint at three tints and
; keep the pixels that changed in BOTH transitions: only terrain responds to
; `grass_tint`, so that set is terrain by construction, it is the SAME pixel set
; in every capture, and it needs no threshold anyone can argue with. The captures
; are bit-reproducible -- the harness settles a fixed number of frames, so the
; wind phase is deterministic and sprite pixels land identically run to run, which
; is the property the whole method rests on. ~246k pixels in rows 400-700.
;
; THE MODEL IS FILMIC WITH ONE CONSTANT PER CHANNEL: screen = filmic(K * tint),
; K folding texture mean and lighting together. Fit against two captures it agreed
; with itself to 1.4 / 5.8 / 7.2 percent, and it EXTRAPOLATES safely, which the
; free-exponent power law fitted here previously did not -- that one only held
; between its two measured points because its exponents were absorbing filmic's
; curvature instead of modelling it. K = 0.0114 / 0.0296 / 0.0079 at this exposure.
;
; THE TEXTURE IS NOW A DRAWN ONE, `TEX_idealized_grass_256px`, and it makes the
; tuft match EASIER rather than undoing it. Its own chromaticity is already near
; the sprites', because it was drawn as grass rather than photographed as forest
; floor; at a plain level-matching neutral tint the ground rendered at red 0.245
; against the photo's 0.202, with the tufts at 0.327. The swap therefore targets
; the shipped ground's rendered colour EXACTLY, so the new texture contributes its
; structure and nothing else, and the hue match is preserved by construction:
;
;   tuft sprites, rendered chromaticity            0.327 0.567 0.106
;   ground, forest_ground_01 @ 9.0 (was)           0.301 0.600 0.099   luma 0.2517
;   ground, idealized_grass @ 3.0 (now)            0.303 0.596 0.101   luma 0.2499
;
; Level is preserved to 0.7 percent, which is the rule the rest of this file is
; built on. (The chromaticities above are measured over the difference mask, which
; is mostly SUNLIT ground; an earlier note in this file quoted 0.297 0.592 0.111
; for the same sprites off a smaller hand-picked patch. Different region, not a
; different result -- but do not mix the two sets when comparing.)
;
; SCALES ARE IN METRES PER TILE. EVERY LAYER RUNS AT 3.0 EXCEPT ROCK, which
; stays at 12.0, and that is a deliberate departure from how this file used to
; set them: each was picked against its own feature size (a mower stripe covering
; about a mower's width, a rock slab covering a slab), which is why they used to
; range from 4.0 to 12.0. Matching them costs the per-layer framing and buys a
; surface whose detail does not change size as one material blends into the next
; -- the splat crossfades between layers over a few metres, and a 4x scale step
; across that blend reads as the ground changing distance rather than changing
; material.
;
; ROCK IS THE ONE LAYER THAT CANNOT TAKE IT, and it fails loudly rather than
; subtly. dark_rock_02 is a photo of FRACTURED SLABS -- it has one dominant
; feature at one size, and a cliff is a single unbroken surface tens of metres
; across with nothing planted on it to interrupt the eye. Take it to 3.0 and the
; slabs shrink to brick size and the tile boundary becomes a visible grid: the
; whole cliff face reads as a masonry wall. The other six layers survive the same
; change because they are either near-isotropic noise (soil, sand, forest litter)
; or are covered in scattered vegetation that breaks the period up. Feature size
; is the rule; the rock is where the rule still bites.
;
; This is also why rock is the layer where scale interacts with `triplanar_
; sharpness` -- it is the only one sampled triplanar, so its tile grid appears on
; three planes at once and a repeat that would be forgivable on flat ground is
; three times as legible on a cliff.
;
; WHAT THIS DOES NOT MATCH IS TEXEL DENSITY, and that is the number that actually
; governs sharpness. Only the grass is 256 px; every other layer is a 128 px
; texture, so at a shared 3.0 they land at 42.7 texel/m against the grass's 85.3
; and read softer than it up close. `textures/` ships 512 px variants of all six
; -- golf, soil, forest, path, rock, sand -- which at 3.0 would give 170.7, i.e.
; twice the grass rather than half. Neither lands on it exactly; 256 px would,
; and none of the six has one. Swapping the 512s in is the fix if the soft layers
; bother you, at 16x the texture memory each.
;
; GRASS RUNS AT 3.0 RATHER THAN THE 9.0 THE PHOTO USED, and the target is the
; POST-PIXELATION resolution, not realism. `post_dither` chunks the frame down
; before anything reaches the screen, which leaves roughly 100 screen px per metre
; of near ground; 256 px over 3 m is ~85 texel/m, so the texture arrives at about
; 1:1 with the pixel grid it is being drawn into. Above that the blades smear into
; brushstrokes (4.5 still reads as strokes, 9.0 as flat mush -- the photo could
; carry 9.0 only because it had no directional feature to smear); below it the
; detail is thrown away by the chunking and the mid field stipples into noise,
; which is what 2.0 does. Changing the pixelation factor is what would move this
; number, not changing the camera.

; CANDY LAND, at the user's request: ISLAND 0's terrain material with the
; candy pack's ground on it (godot/island/candy/textures): candy grass for the
; turf, the forest mosaic under the woods, purple-and-yellow tiles for bare earth,
; turquoise-and-pink for the shore, the brown candy cliff for rock, grey
; cobblestone for the roads (the path layer) and rainbow tiles for the town
; squares (the build pads: `build_paving`). No normal maps: the pack has none,
; and the photo set's would put pebbles under the tiles. Tints 1: the pack is
; already graded bright. The fog is the sky's blue.
[resource]
render_priority = 0
shader = ExtResource("1_w2")
shader_parameter/grass_albedo = ExtResource("2_grass_a")
shader_parameter/grass_scale = 4.0
shader_parameter/grass_tint = Color(1, 1, 1, 1)
shader_parameter/golf_albedo = ExtResource("4_golf_a")
shader_parameter/golf_scale = 4.0
shader_parameter/golf_tint = Color(1, 1, 1, 1)
shader_parameter/soil_albedo = ExtResource("5_soil_a")
shader_parameter/soil_scale = 4.0
shader_parameter/soil_tint = Color(1, 1, 1, 1)
shader_parameter/forest_albedo = ExtResource("7_forest_a")
shader_parameter/forest_scale = 4.0
shader_parameter/forest_tint = Color(1, 1, 1, 1)
shader_parameter/forest_grass_mix = 0.75
shader_parameter/path_albedo = ExtResource("8_path_a")
shader_parameter/path_scale = 3.0
shader_parameter/path_tint = Color(1.1, 1.1, 1.15, 1)
shader_parameter/rock_albedo = ExtResource("9_rock_a")
shader_parameter/rock_scale = 8.0
shader_parameter/rock_tint = Color(1.2, 1.2, 1.2, 1)
; THE STONE UP THE SHOULDER (at the user's request: the stone right up to
; where the slope at the island's edge ends, then a fast change to grass):
; the cliff wall's own stone, at MAT_candy_cliff's rock numbers, on any
; ground steeper than 0.30 (1 - normal.y), whole by 0.36
shader_parameter/steep_stone = 1.0
shader_parameter/steep_lo = 0.3
shader_parameter/steep_hi = 0.36
shader_parameter/stone_albedo = ExtResource("20_stone")
shader_parameter/stone_scale = 8.0
shader_parameter/stone_tint = Color(1.2, 1.2, 1.2, 1)
shader_parameter/stone_mean = Color(0.036, 0.04, 0.045, 1)
shader_parameter/stone_contrast = 0.7
; and down the side it goes over to the cloud sea's darkest grey: from 40 m
; over the sea (the lip is ~45 m over it) to 14 m, MAT_candy_cliff's numbers
shader_parameter/deep_on = true
shader_parameter/deep_color = Color(0.42, 0.47, 0.55, 1)
shader_parameter/deep_top = 40.0
shader_parameter/deep_bottom = 14.0
shader_parameter/triplanar_sharpness = 4.0
shader_parameter/sand_albedo = ExtResource("11_sand_a")
shader_parameter/sand_scale = 4.0

; THE CRASH SITE. Two layers, and they are the only two in this file that are
; GENERATED rather than photographed -- `textures/proc/TOOL_gen_crater_textures.py`
; bakes them from `dirt_01` and `dark_rock_02`, the very layers they replace, so
; a burnt patch reads as this ground burnt rather than as a different material
; appearing at the crater's edge.
;
; WHICH CHANGES HOW THE TINTS ARE SET, and it is the one place this file's method
; does not apply. Every other row above solves a tint to move a photograph's mean
; onto a target it was never shot for -- three numbers, because a photo has a hue
; nobody chose. These two were baked to their hue, so their tints are NEUTRAL and
; do nothing but set level. A per-channel grade here would be correcting a colour
; this repo picked on purpose, and the right place to change it is the bake.
;
;   layer   texture mean (lin RGB)  tint   graded luma   what it sits against
;   char    0.0087 0.0056 0.0033    1.75      0.0107     soil graded at 0.0921
;   slag    0.0083 0.0061 0.0045    1.80      0.0117     rock graded at 0.0987
;
; The texture means are PRINTED BY THE BAKE, in linear, which is the space this
; table is written in -- `TOOL_gen_crater_textures.py` reports them on every run for
; exactly that reason. Quoting an encoded mean and grading against a linear one
; is the slip that would otherwise be invisible here.
;
; MUCH DARKER THAN THE PREVIOUS PASS, ON BOTH SIDES OF THE MULTIPLY. Those were
; 0.0198 and 0.0275 graded -- a fifth and better than a quarter of the layer each
; replaces -- and the crash site read as a pale scar on a dark island, which is
; the one direction a burn must never go. They now sit near 12% each, roughly
; half what they were, and BOTH the bake and the tint came down to get there.
; Which way round that goes is the whole method: tint alone would have crushed
; fourteen quantisation steps toward each other to buy the level, and bake alone
; would have been undone by the grade. Neither clips.
;
; DARKNESS IS ALSO WHAT FIXED THE HUE, which is not obvious and cost several
; renders to establish. The char kept reading as dirt in shadow rather than as
; charcoal, and the natural move -- desaturate the bake -- makes it WORSE:
; `ps1-soft`'s dark near-neutrals are mostly olive, so a greyer dark brown lands
; on green and the lip comes back mossy. What works is getting under the band
; where the palette is saturated at all. See CARBON in the bake for the numbers.
;
; THE CHAR'S FIRST NUMBERS WERE 0.45x SOIL AND IT RENDERED AS A SAND QUARRY --
; which, with a bunker layer in the same shader, is the one thing it must not
; look like. Two causes, both fixed in the BAKE rather than here: the burn floor
; was leaving a fifth of the dirt's own brown showing through, and the ash was
; #a0a0a0 over 55% of the surface, which under this sun is white glitter.
;
; THE 6.6x THAT ONCE CONSTRAINED THE SLAG BAKE NO LONGER DOES, and it is worth
; saying why rather than quietly dropping it. That number came from a dark plate
; being asked to hit a BRIGHT target: hold the target and a dark texture needs an
; enormous multiplier, which stretches a 14-level texture's steps over six times
; their width. The target moved, so the plate got the value it always wanted
; (#4a3826 -> #282018) and the tint went DOWN with it. The constraint was never
; on the colour; it was on the pair.
;
; BOTH TEXTURES ARE NOW THREE SUB-VARIANTS EACH -- a plate with two others
; blotted into it, which is why their means moved even where a colour did not.
; The bake owns that composition entirely; nothing in this file needs to know
; about it beyond re-reading the printed mean after a re-bake.
shader_parameter/char_albedo = ExtResource("13_char_a")
; 3.0 like every other ground layer, because charred dirt blends into soil and
; grass at the ash apron and a scale step across that blend reads as the ground
; changing distance rather than changing material.
shader_parameter/char_scale = 3.0
shader_parameter/char_tint = Color(1.75, 1.75, 1.75, 1)
shader_parameter/slag_albedo = ExtResource("14_slag_a")
; 12.0, MATCHING ROCK EXACTLY, and it is the one crater number set against a
; junction rather than against a feature size. The slag rides the rock channel, so
; where the crater fades out its texture crossfades into `dark_rock_02` on the
; same channel at the same texel -- a scale step there would be a seam in a single
; surface. The step it does keep, against char at 3.0, is the soil/rock step this
; island already has everywhere.
;
; It also happens to be right for the crack network: the bake's plates are an
; eighth of a tile, so 12 m puts them at 1.5 m across. At 3.0 they would be 0.4 m
; and the floor would read as lizard skin.
shader_parameter/slag_scale = 12.0
shader_parameter/slag_tint = Color(1.80, 1.80, 1.80, 1)
; THE GLOWING CRACKS, and this is the dial to reach for first. It multiplies the
; ramp and is meant to exceed 1.0 -- the Environment's `glow_enabled` blooms
; anything over it, which is the difference between cracks that are lit and cracks
; that are radiating. Chosen against a filmic tonemap and a 0.25 sun and NOT yet
; against the engine; the same caveat `ember_energy` carries in
; docs/DOC_burnt_vegetation.md applies here for the same reason.
shader_parameter/crack_glow = 2.6
; The vegetation's ember ramp, unchanged and unduplicated. `post_dither` snaps
; the frame to sixteen entries from the same palette these eight came from, so a
; second fire ramp would be quantised onto this one anyway.
shader_parameter/crack_ramp = ExtResource("15_crack_ramp")
; Slower than the plants' 1.6. A burning fir is a fire and flickers; a crater
; floor is a cooling mass and breathes.
shader_parameter/crack_cycle_speed = 1.1
shader_parameter/crack_cycle_depth = 0.42
shader_parameter/crack_cycle_scatter = 3.5
; THE MOLTEN CORE, gated on `IslandField.crater_molten` — the inner 30% of the
; radius, which sits inside the flat floor. These three are what make the middle
; of the crater a different thing from the rest of it rather than the same crust
; with a brighter light on it.
;
; `melt_gamma` is the one to reach for. It is an EXPONENT on the baked heat, so
; it widens the crack network instead of brightening it: 0.55 takes a seam texel
; at 0.03 up to 0.15, so the threads swell to ribbons and the cooled rafts stand
; out between them. 1.0 switches the whole core off and leaves the floor uniform.
; The first pass ran it at 0.30 and the crater floor came back a gold lava lake
; from rim to rim -- past about 0.4 the rafts close over and the pool stops being
; a pool and becomes a disc, which is the failure to watch for.
shader_parameter/melt_gamma = 0.55
; Additive on top of `crack_glow`, so the pool sits over the Environment's bloom
; threshold where the outer seams sit under it.
shader_parameter/melt_glow = 0.9
shader_parameter/melt_shade = 0.35
; ---- the escape pod's divot -------------------------------------------------
;
; The dent's ground is GRADED RATHER THAN SAMPLED, because there is no seventeenth
; texture unit to sample it with; the shader's `divot` block carries that argument
; in full. What is here is the grade, and it is anchored to the soil layer's
; measured level rather than chosen: `TEX_dirt_01_albedo_128.png` averages 0.121
; linear luminance and takes `soil_tint` 1.55, so the churned earth is that same
; ground at 0.82 of its level -- freshly opened dirt read against the dry crust
; over it, which is the one comparison the eye actually makes here. Both surfaces
; are in frame at once along the whole rim of the dent, so the difference between
; them is the entire effect and its absolute level is not.
;
; 0.82 / 0.74 / 0.66 rather than a neutral: turned ground is WARMER as well as
; darker, because what is being exposed is subsoil rather than shaded topsoil.
; A neutral darkening read as the dent being in shadow, which is exactly the
; reading to avoid on a scene with no shadows in it.
shader_parameter/divot_scale = 1.7
shader_parameter/divot_tint = Color(0.82, 0.74, 0.66, 1)
shader_parameter/divot_break = 0.45
shader_parameter/divot_grit = 0.3
; Paler and drier than weathered cliff, and only just: `dark_rock_02` is already
; near-black (0.036 linear) and carries a 3.4 tint to get off the floor, so a
; scarp grade with any real range in it lands the cut face in a different palette
; cell from the rock a metre away and reads as a decal rather than as a cut.
shader_parameter/divot_scarp_tint = Color(1.08, 1.05, 1, 1)
shader_parameter/normal_strength = 0.6
; ZERO NOW THE ISLAND IS UNLIT. `1 - (1 - n.y) * k` was a hand-rolled stand-in for
; the sky/ground hemisphere in `SHADERINC_island_light.gdshaderinc`, which does the same job
; properly, continuously, and in colour rather than in level — leaving both on
; double-counts the one axis they share. Kept as a knob, not deleted: it is still
; the cheapest way to push relief around if the hemisphere needs help.
shader_parameter/slope_shade = 0.0

; THE UNLIT RESPONSE CURVE. Colours are NOT here — they are the four `tod_*`
; globals, so the day/night cycle moves the whole island without touching a
; material. These four are the shape of the response, and `MAT_cliff_wall.tres`
; MUST carry the same values: the wall continues this surface across a shared ring
; in plain sight, so a different curve either side is a seam in the shading exactly
; where the albedo was made seamless. tests/TEST_island_world.gd asserts they agree.
;
; FOUR BANDS on the sun term, not a smooth ramp, and that is the palette's doing.
; `post_dither` snaps the frame to sixteen entries; a smooth ramp handed to a
; quantiser comes back as contour lines wherever the entries happen to fall, plus
; crawling Bayer noise as the camera moves. Posterising to a count we choose puts
; the bands where we want them and lets them read as cel shading.
shader_parameter/sun_bands = 4.0
; 0.35 of wrap past the terminator. At 0 the unlit side drops to pure ambient in
; one step, which on banded shading draws a hard ring right round every hill.
shader_parameter/sun_wrap = 0.45
shader_parameter/rim_power = 3.0
; The sky-tinted fresnel edge — what makes an unlit landform read as having volume
; against a void sky. Low: at silhouette strength it outlines the terrain like a
; decal instead of catching light on it.
shader_parameter/rim_strength = 0.25

; THE ENVIRONMENT BALL. Baked by `scripts/SCRIPT_bake_env_ball.gd` against this scene's
; actual sky (MAT_sky_w2_void.tres) and cloud deck, and sampled by the world normal
; in place of the analytic sky/ground lerp. It stores WEIGHTS, not colours — the
; day/night bus still supplies every colour — so it costs one fetch and survives the
; cycle. See the shader include for what each channel means.
;
; MUST MATCH MAT_cliff_wall.tres. The wall below the shoulder continues this
; surface across a shared ring in plain sight, so an ambient model that differs
; either side of it is a seam in the shading exactly where the albedo was made
; seamless. tests/TEST_env_ball.gd asserts both materials bind the same ball.
shader_parameter/env_ball_enabled = true
shader_parameter/env_ball = ExtResource("99_envball")
shader_parameter/env_ball_night = ExtResource("99_envballn")
shader_parameter/flat_shading = false
shader_parameter/fog_enabled = true
shader_parameter/fog_color = Color(0.62, 0.8, 1, 1)
shader_parameter/fog_top = -170.0
shader_parameter/fog_bottom = -320.0
shader_parameter/fog_curve = 2.2
shader_parameter/fog_opacity_gain = 1.6
shader_parameter/fog_distance_start = 850.0
shader_parameter/fog_distance_density = 0.0012
shader_parameter/fog_distance_power = 2.0
shader_parameter/sand_tint = Color(1, 1, 1, 1)
shader_parameter/build_paving = 1.0
shader_parameter/build_albedo = ExtResource("4_golf_a")
